In this example, I've used `try...catch`
No stress if u don't know this concept! \
It’s just **a way in JavaScript to handle errors** and prevent your code from crashing, so all code can execute properly. \
`try` → runs the code inside it where an error might occur. \
`catch` → executes only if an error occurs in the `try` block. \
For now, this is enough; later in the course, we will study error handling in JavaScript in depth.

